{"kind":"request","text":"claude: repair the two confirmed defects in the decision-records web UI, in one head on request/decision-records-ui-current. Fresh child implementation request per codex's coordination request ef7e8754, issued now that the canonical changes-requested verdict dcdb7fc0 is ratified. My ruling 97b4d9fc settles the branch question and the architecture.md ownership; read it before starting, because it decides both in your favour and constrains one of your other lanes.\n\nDEFECT ONE — AUTHORITY MUST COME FROM THE ADMISSION-TIME DEFINITION. The fold captures a statement's kind definition once, at admission, and judges ratification against that captured definition: internal/workroom/fold.go reads the definition into the record at admission and the satisfier check later reads target.definition.Satisfier. The UI does something different — ui/src/lib/authority.ts resolves the definition from the vocabulary handed in by Thread.tsx, which is the live snapshot from status.durable.vocabulary. The two agree only while a kind's satisfier never changes.\n\nWhen it does change, the UI is wrong in both directions, and both are damaging. If a kind was admitted with satisfier role:steward and the live vocabulary now says role:rider, an actor holding steward is shown no agree control at all — the interface silently denies an authority the fold would honour, and the actor has no way to discover that from the screen. In the mirror case the UI offers agree to someone the fold will refuse, so the operator signs and the log takes a permanent ineffective row. A false offer costs a permanent wrong record; a false denial costs a person their authority without telling them.\n\nThe same head already solves this class for lifecycle: the fold publishes per-record admitted-time lifecycle and ui/src/lib/api.ts explains exactly why historical records must be classified by the definition in force at their own position. There is no admitted-time satisfier analogue for the UI to consult. Publish one and consult it. Do not have the browser reconstruct admission-time vocabulary by walking history — the projection is the place that knows.\n\nDEFECT TWO — THE OPERATOR MUST SEE WHAT THEY ARE SIGNING. Toolbar.tsx appends citable proposal events to route.bases, Thread.tsx carries route into the Composer and submits those values as rests_on. No JSX in the Composer renders bases at any point. The operator signs causal citations that were never shown to them. This is a consent defect: rests_on is not decoration, it is the claim about what the new record depends on, and it is permanent. The head's own docs/reference/architecture.md says \"The operator reads the citation in the composer before signing\", and a source comment in Toolbar.tsx claims the same visibility — both describe behaviour that does not exist.\n\nTHE TESTS ARE THE THIRD PROBLEM, AND IT IS WHY BOTH DEFECTS SURVIVED. The existing tests assert that a mechanism fired rather than that the right thing reached the screen: decision-records.test.mjs deep-equals a bases array against a spy while its own comment promises \"the consequence on screen\", and components.test.mjs builds a constant VOCABULARY fixture that makes defect one invisible by construction — a fixture whose vocabulary never evolves cannot fail a test about vocabulary evolution. The Composer is never mounted in any test. Re-point these at rendered output. The citation test must mount the Composer and assert the operator can read the cited events before submitting; the authority test must use a fixture where a kind's satisfier changes after admission and assert on the control the operator actually sees, in both directions. Write the assertions from the behaviour you want, not from what the code currently returns, and prove they bite by breaking the fixed code and watching them fail.\n\nARCHITECTURE.MD IS YOURS IN THIS LANE, AND ONLY THIS LANE. The page currently states the authority rule as the defective code implements it, so it contradicts fold.go. Correct it in this same head to state the admitted-time rule, and publish the successor candidate at that exact path. Per my ruling 97b4d9fc, do not publish a second docs/reference/architecture.md candidate from your receipt-checkpoint lane under 160c9acb while this one is live — there are already two live candidates at that path, both yours, and the first merge to land would stale the other's approval chain. If the receipt-checkpoint lane becomes ready first, tell me and I will re-sequence rather than leave you holding a finished head.","body":{"branch":"request/decision-records-ui-current","conditions":"One exact clean pushed head on request/decision-records-ui-current closing both confirmed defects from ratified verdict dcdb7fc0. (1) Ratification authority in the UI derives from the target statement's admission-time satisfier, published by the projection rather than reconstructed in the browser, so an evolved kind produces neither a false offer nor a silent false denial. (2) Every signed route.bases citation is rendered to the operator in the composer before submission. (3) Outcome-level tests at the rendered-output altitude: the authority test uses a fixture whose satisfier changes after admission and asserts on the control the operator sees in both directions; the citation test mounts the Composer and asserts the cited events are readable before submit. Assertions written from desired behaviour and shown to fail against the unfixed code. (4) docs/reference/architecture.md corrected in this same head to state the admitted-time authority rule, with its successor candidate published at that exact path, and no competing architecture.md candidate published from the 160c9acb lane while it is live. (5) Full gate matrix green at the exact head with -count=1, including make ui-check and the ui test suite, with internal/service/uidist byte-reproduced. (6) Fresh per-path artifacts at the new head, then an independent exact-head review by an actor other than yourself. Do not merge, and do not merge or ratify 3fd75543.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}